  <page>
    <title>Perth, Ontario</title>
    <id>83395</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42049930</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T13:22:12Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>209.217.69.130</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[image:lanpth.PNG|right]]

'''Perth''' is a [[town]] in eastern [[Ontario]], [[Canada]] (pop. 6,003 in [[2001]]). It is located on the [[Tay River (Ontario)|Tay River]], 83 km southwest of [[Ottawa]], and is the seat of [[Lanark County, Ontario|Lanark County]].

The town was established as a military settlement in [[1816]], shortly after the [[War of 1812]]. Many of the first settlers were military veterans on [[half pay]],others were immigrants from [[Scotland]] or [[Ireland]].Many of the Scottish immigrants were [[stonemason]]s; their work can be seen in many area buildings and in the locks of the [[Rideau Canal]].  

Near the town is the home of world [[show jumping]] champion [[Ian Millar]] and Millar Brooke Farm where his great horse [[Big Ben (horse)|Big Ben]] (1976-1999) is buried. The town has erected a bronze life-sized statue of the horse and Ian Millar, across from the Code's Mill building.

This town was the site of the last fatal [[duel]] in the province. [[Robert Lyon (duel)|Robert Lyon]], a law student, was killed on [[June 13]] [[1833]] after an argument with a former friend, John Wilson. 

Perth is also the site of the first installation of a telephone other than Bell's experimental installations, a town doctor and friend of [[Alexander Graham Bell]] installed a direct telephone connection between his home and office.

An interesting feature of the downtown core is the Crystal Palace, constructed from the remnants of the glass street
enclosures that used to be on [[Rideau Street (Ottawa)|Rideau Street]] in nearby Ottawa.  This building on the Tay Basin is the location of the very popular [http://www.urbanmarket.com/all-about-perth/fmarket.html Perth Farmers' Market].

The heritage downtown core of today's Perth now boasts of over 80 unique stores, boutiques, specialty shops and restaurants, including crafts, antiques and flea market,
for the visitor looking for alternatives to the big city shopping malls. Most of these operate out of the century old stone buildings in town. The drugstore on Foster Street in the downtown core (now called Perth Pharmasave, formerly Girdwood's Drug Store) is the olderst continually operating pharmacy in Canada.
PDF versions of the visitors guide is available for printing at:
[http://www.urbanmarket.com/all-about-perth/brochure.pdf Your Quick Guide to the Merchants of Downtown Perth] such as [[Valley book shop]], 
[http://maps.google.com/maps?q=perth+ontario&amp;spn=93.515625,101.425781&amp;t=k&amp;hl=en GIS Map of Perth]

According to the [[2001]] [[Statistics Canada]] Census:
*% Change ([[1996]]-[[2001]]): 1.7
*Dwellings: 2,953
*Area (sq. km.): 10.36
*Density (persons per sq. km.): 579.2

==See also==
[[Perth, Scotland]]
==External links==
*[http://www.town.perth.on.ca/ Official Site]
*[http://shop-heritage-perth.com  BIA Site]
*[http://all-about-perth.com all-about-perth.com]
*[http://www.exploreperth.com Explore Perth]
*[http://www.valleybookshop.com Valley Book Shop] A Great Place for Books

&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;table width = 75% border = 2 align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td width = 35% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;
&lt;td width = 30% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;'''North:''' [[Drummond/North Elmsley, Ontario|Drummond/North Elmsley]]&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width = 35% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td width = 10% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;'''West:''' [[Tay Valley, Ontario|Tay Valley]] &lt;br&gt; &lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width = 35% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;'''Perth'''&lt;br&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width = 30% align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;'''East:''' [[Drummond/North Elmsley, Ontario|Drummond/North Elmsley]]&lt;/td&gt;
