  <page>
    <title>Crack intro</title>
    <id>83465</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41954407</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T21:29:50Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Hoovernj</username>
        <id>122559</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">A '''crack intro''', also known as a '''cracktro''', '''loader''', or just '''intro''', is a small introduction sequence added to [[Software cracking|cracked software]], designed to inform the user which &quot;cracking crew&quot; or individual cracker was responsible for removing the software's [[copy prevention]] and distributing the crack.

These first appeared on [[Apple II family|Apple II]] and later [[Commodore 64]] [[computer game|games]] that were distributed around the world via [[Bulletin Board System]]s (BBSes) and [[floppy disk]] copying. Initially the intros consisted of simple messages, but they grew progressively more complex as (the programmers from) each cracking crew attempted to show off how much more talented they were than (other programmers in) other groups.

As a result, crack intros began to feature big colourful [[2D computer graphics|effects]], [[chiptune|music]], and [[scrolling|scrollers]]. Cracking groups would use the intros not just to gain credit for cracking, but to advertise their BBSes, greet friends, and to give themselves recognition. Messages were frequently of a [[vulgar]] nature, and on some occasions made threats of violence against software companies.

Crack intro programming eventually became an art form in its own right, and people started coding intros without attaching them to a crack just to show off how well they could program. This evolved into the [[demoscene]].

==External links==
[[Image:Bpinballm2.png|right|thumb|193px|A classic example of an early Apple IIe crack intro screen; ''Beyond Pinball'' cracked by Brian Fist (circa [[1986]]).]]
* [http://cracktros.planet-d.net World of Cracktros] &amp;ndash; A site containing [[Amiga]] crack intros removed from illegal cracks and enhanced for modern Amigas and [[emulator]]s
* [http://www.intros.c64.org World of C64 Crackintros] &amp;ndash; A large collection of C64 cracktros in native 'prg' file format (supported by most C64 emulators)
* [http://www.flashtro.com Flashtros] &amp;ndash; Amiga cracktros converted to [[Macromedia Flash|Flash]] format
* [http://www.docsnyderspage.de DocSnydersPage] &amp;ndash; Collection of C64 cracktros recoded in [[Macromedia Flash|Flash]]
* [http://www.pouet.net Pouet] &amp;ndash; A [[demoscene]] archive including cracktros for all types of operating systems
