|[[Japan]] || [[Image:DP red.png]] Yes || [[September 16]] [[2005]] &lt;sup&gt;[http://www.japantimes.co.jp/cgi-bin/getarticle.pl5?nn20050917a2.htm]&lt;/sup&gt; || 2 || Prosecutors only push for the death penalty in the case of multiple murders, or if a single murder is combined with either rape or robbery. &lt;sup&gt;[http://www.washingtonpost.com/ac2/wp-dyn/A11306-2005Jan15?language=printer]&lt;/sup&gt; || Only method of capital punishment used in Japan today is [[hanging]]. Between 1946 and 2003, Japanese courts sentenced 766 people to death, 608 of whom were executed. A moratorium was placed on executions for 40 months from [[1989]] to [[1993]].  As of the end of 2004, there were 118 convicted criminals under sentence of death in Japan. Sixty-eight of them  had their convictions and sentences affirmed on appeal and were subject to execution at any time. No execution dates are given in advance. Rather, inmates learn that they’re to be executed when a guard comes one morning and gives them the news. [http://www.policyreview.org/aug05/lane.html]
|-
|[[Jordan]] || [[Image:DP red.png]] Yes || 2005 &lt;sup&gt;[http://www.richard.clark32.btinternet.co.uk/jun05.html]&lt;/sup&gt; || 1 &lt;sup&gt;[http://web.amnesty.org/library/Index/ENGACT500052005?open&amp;of=ENG-392]&lt;/sup&gt; || Murder &lt;sup&gt;[http://www.richard.clark32.btinternet.co.uk/aug03.html]&lt;/sup&gt; || 
