  <page>
    <title>Château de Saumur</title>
    <id>83504</id>
    <revision>
      <id>33751115</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-03T19:08:10Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Luc Pepin</username>
        <id>447961</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Chauteau Saumur 2.jpg|The chateau at Saumur|thumb|300px|right]]

Located in the [[France|French]] town of [[Saumur]], in the [[Maine-et-Loire]] département, the '''Château de Saumur''' was originally constructed as a fortified stronghold.  After its destruction in [[1067]], the [[château]] was rebuilt by a member of the powerful [[Plantagenet]] family.

In the early part of the 1200s, [[Philip II of France|King Philippe II]] made Saumur part of his royal domain but his son, [[Louis IX of France|King Louis IX]] (Saint Louis) was responsible for the castle's rebirth. It changed hands several times until 1589 when the Protestant [[Henry IV of France|King Henri IV (of France and Navarre)]] gifted the castle to Duplessis-Mornay. 

In [[1621]] the castle was converted into an army barracks then into a state prison under [[Napoleon Bonaparte]]. The castle has a dungeon and watchtower, and houses the ''Musée de la Figurine-Jouet'', a collection of very old toys and [[figurine]]s of soldiers, kings of France, and clowns.

In the first part of the 20th century, the city of Saumur acquired the castle and began a restoration program to house the museum of the decorative arts. In line with the Saumur area's equestrian tradition and its famous &quot;[[Cadre Noir]]&quot;,  the castle also serves as a Museum of the Horse.

[[Category:Castles in France|Saumur]]
[[Category:Official historical monuments of France]]
[[Category:Maine-et-Loire]]

[[fr:Château de Saumur]]
[[it:Castello di Saumur]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Fanny Coot</title>
    <id>83505</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36018448</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-20T23:03:40Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>96T</username>
        <id>97147</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Fanny Coot''' is a [[fictional character]]  of the [[Scrooge McDuck universe]]. She was first mentioned in the [[Donald Duck]] [[comic strip]] of [[May 9]], [[1938]] where [[Gus Goose]] first appeared. She is Gus's mother and [[Donald Duck]]'s father's cousin. In [[Don Rosa]]'s Duck Family Tree she is featured as a daughter of [[Casey Coot]] and his wife [[Gretchin Grebe | Gretchen Grebe]],  and so a niece of [[Elvira Coot]], Donald's paternal grandmother. She also had a brother named [[Cuthbert Coot]]. Fanny married [[Luke the Goose]] and so became the mother of Gus Goose.

[[Category:Characters in the Scrooge McDuck universe|Coot, Fanny]]

[[it:Fanny Coot]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Casey Coot</title>
    <id>83513</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36083588</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-21T13:06:22Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>96T</username>
        <id>97147</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Casey Coot''' is a [[fictional character]] of the [[Scrooge McDuck universe]] created by [[Don Rosa]]. He first appeared in &quot;Last Sled to Dawson&quot;, first published in [[June]], [[1988]]. He is introduced as an unsuccessful gold prospector and friend of [[Scrooge McDuck]] during his years in [[Klondike, Yukon|Klondike]]. In need of money he sold to the significantly more successful [[Scrooge McDuck]] his share in [[Duckburg]], [[Calisota]], [[United States|USA]]. His share included &quot;Killmule Hill&quot; which renamed to &quot;Killmotor Hill&quot; comprises the land where Scrooge's money bin stands on. He later appeared in ''[[The Life and Times of Scrooge McDuck]] Part 8'' and ''Hearts of the Yukon''. In ''The Life and Times of Scrooge McDuck Part 10'' it is revealed that he and [[Grandma Duck]] are brother and sister. 

In [[Don Rosa]]'s Duck Family Tree he is featured as a grandson of [[Cornelius Coot]], a son of [[Clinton Coot]] and his wife [[Gertrude Gadwall]]. According to the tree he was married to [[Gretchin Grebe | Gretchen Grebe]] and they had at least two kids named [[Fanny Coot]] and [[Cuthbert Coot]]. He is also the grandfather of [[Gus Goose]]. It has been suggested that he is also the grandfather of [[Kildare Coot]] although Rosa has not used the later in his stories or tree.

According to Don Rosa´s unofficial timelines, Casey Coot was born in [[1860]] in [[Duckburg]], [[Calisota]] and died sometimes before [[1970]].

[[Category:Characters in the Scrooge McDuck universe|Coot, Casey]]

[[fr:Jules Écoutum]]
[[it:Casey Coot]]
[[nl:Koenraad Prul]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Orphans of the Sky</title>
    <id>83516</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36869276</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-27T00:17:38Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Alba</username>
        <id>34312</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:OrphansOfTheSky1964hardback.JPG|thumb|1964 hardback edition]]

'''Orphans of the Sky''' is a [[1951]] [[science fiction]] [[novella]] by [[Robert A. Heinlein]], consisting of two parts: &quot;Universe&quot; and &quot;Common Sense.&quot; The two parts were originally published as a two part serial in ''[[Astounding Science Fiction]]'' magazine in 1941. &quot;Universe&quot; was also published separately as a Dell book in 1951.

In the novella, the gigantic, cylindrical [[generation ship]] ''Vanguard'' is cruising pilotless toward a distant [[solar system]], inhabited by a crew that has forgotten their origins over time and lapsed into superstition.  Most crew members lead a simple bucolic life of [[agriculture|farming]], never venturing to the upper decks where the &quot;muties&quot; ([[mutant]]s or [[mutiny | mutineers]]) dwell.  One day, Hugh Hoyland starts exploring and finds the astonishing secret of the ship's purpose.

This story refers to the sister ship ''New Frontiers'', the ship stolen by the Howard families in the story ''[[Methuselah's Children]]''. This ship and the aftermath of this story are mentioned in passing in the novel ''[[Time Enough for Love]]''.

{{sf-book-stub}}

==Printings==
* Hardcover, 1964 [[G. P. Putnam's Sons]]
* Hardcover, January 2001, ISBN 1588810070
* Paperback, December 2001, ISBN 0671318454

[[category:1964 books]]
[[Category:Novels by Robert A. Heinlein]]
[[Category:Science fiction books]]

[[fr:Les Orphelins du ciel]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Matriarch</title>
    <id>83518</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38616990</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-07T14:37:04Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Nlu</username>
        <id>350890</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/66.240.8.178|66.240.8.178]] ([[User talk:66.240.8.178|talk]]) to last version by Blackcap</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Move to Wiktionary}}A '''matriarch''' or clan-mother or 'chief' is a [[mother]], or other female person in a family who is responsible for the well-being of the [[clan]] or [[kinship]]. The term is usually applied to the oldest female in an extended family, who by virtue of her position has a degree of granted  authority because others have trust in her. The title can be inherited, mostly by the youngest daughter.
In many matriarchal societies she is responsible mainly for distributing food and also she can be the [[shaman]] of the clan. A matriarch can never rule or govern over the members of the community because matriarchies always decide by [[consensus]].

==See also==
*[[matriarchy]]
*[[patriarch]]


{{Culture-stub}}

[[Category:Society]]</text>
    </revision>
