  <page>
    <title>Egmont</title>
    <id>83546</id>
    <revision>
      <id>15974931</id>
      <timestamp>2005-05-12T23:31:37Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Eugene van der Pijll</username>
        <id>22016</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>tidied up a bit; separate page for play and music; see also [[Wikipedia:Disambiguation/Style]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Egmont''' or '''Egmond''' may refer to the following:

* '''[[Egmond family]]''' (often spelled &quot;Egmont&quot;), an influential Dutch family, lords of the town of Egmond
* '''[[Egmond]]''', a town in North Holland, the Netherlands, named after the family
* '''[[Lamoral, Count of Egmont]]''', the best known member of the Egmont family
* '''''[[Egmont (play)|Egmont]]''''', a play by Goethe, about Lamoral, Count of Egmond; with an overture and incidental music by Beethoven.
* '''[[Egmont (media group)|Egmont]]''' a Scandinavian media group, founded in 1878. &lt;!-- (making the publicity for this firm a bit less prominent - please create a separate page [[Egmont (Media Group)]] or something in this sense if wanting to re-use some of this, but preferably do it more [[NPOV]] then:) They are the biggest Danish media company, with local operations in most European countries and China, see: [http://www.egmont.com visit Egmont]. Egmont creates and tells stories ranging from print to plasma screen, from entertainment to education, from intellectual to popular culture. Egmont encompasses weeklies, magazines, comics, books, film, TV-programmes, textbooks, games, content for mobile services and electronic entertainment. Egmont has activities in 22 countries, and 3,550 Egmont employees reached revenues of EUR 1.107 billion in 2003. The company is entirely held by the Egmont foundation which uses its profits on charity work for the benefit of children. Egmont has five divisions, [[Nordisk Film]], Egmont Magazines, Egmont Books and Egmont Kids &amp; Teens and several strong brands like [[Aschehoug]], [[Damm]] and ALT for Damerne is part of the Group. The [[comics]] publications of Egmont include original work, original work with licensed characters (including [[The Phantom]] and various [[The Walt Disney Company|Disney]] comics), and translations of American comics ([[Marvel]], [[DC Comics]] and [[Dark Horse]]). [[Don Rosa]] used to work for Egmont until he quit to work for [[Picsou Magazine]].--&gt;
*'''Mount Egmont''' is the alternative name for [[Mount Taranaki]] in New Zealand, and gives its name to the [[Egmont National Park]].
*'''[[Egmont (electoral district)|Egmont]]''', a Federal Canadian electoral district

{{disambig}}
[[da:egmont]]
[[fr:Egmont]]</text>
