  <page>
    <title>Argea</title>
    <id>83561</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36244650</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-22T18:39:13Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Brian0918</username>
        <id>90640</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">:''For the doll-like figures used in Ancient Roman ceremonies, see [[Argei]]''.

In [[Greek mythology]], '''Argea''' (or Argeia) was a daughter of King [[Adrastus]] of [[Argos]]. She was married to [[Polynices]], the exiled king of [[Thebes (Greece)|Thebes]].

Sources include:
*[[Apollodorus]] 3.6.1
*[[Euripides]] in ''The Phoenician Women'' and ''Suppliants'', who mentions the wedding without giving her name.
*[[Hyginus]], who in his ''Fabulae'' (Latin) calls her Argia.
*[[Robert Graves]] in his popular ''The Greek Myths'' (106c) prefers the spelling Aegeia.

{{Greek-myth-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Route 21 (New Jersey)</title>
    <id>83562</id>
    <revision>
      <id>30750981</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-09T20:46:37Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>SPUI</username>
        <id>113059</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>shield</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Circle sign 21.svg|right|150px]]
'''Route 21''' is a highway in northern [[New Jersey]]. Its southern terminus is an interchange with [[U.S. Highway 1|US 1]]/[[U.S. Highway 9|US 9]] and [[United States Highway 22|US 22]] in [[Newark, New Jersey]].  Its northern terminus is an interchange with [[U.S. Highway 46|US 46]] in [[Clifton, New Jersey]].

The section of highway through Newark is known as McCarter Highway.  It parallels the [[Pennsylvania Railroad]] tracks up to [[Pennsylvania Station (Newark)|Newark Penn Station]].  This portion was commissioned in [[1934]].

Between [[1955]] and [[1959]], the highway was extended northward as a freeway along the west bank of the [[Passaic River]] to an interchange with [[Route 3 (New Jersey)|NJ 3]].  The road made it to the southern border between Clifton and [[Passaic, New Jersey|Passaic]] by [[1961]].  An extension to Paulison Avenue in Passaic opened in [[1970]], and a section to Monroe Street opened in [[1974]]. For this last section, the river was rerouted, and the road clips [[Wallington, New Jersey|Wallington]] in [[Bergen County, New Jersey|Bergen County]] without crossing today's river.

For a quarter-century, traffic headed for [[Paterson, New Jersey]] was dumped onto local streets in Passaic.  Finally in [[1998]], construction began on the &quot;missing link&quot; of the freeway, which opened [[December 22]], [[2000]].

Sections of McCarter Highway are being improved [[as of 2004]], where the road passes features such as [[Bears and Eagles Riverfront Stadium]], the [[New Jersey Performing Arts Center]], and a new arena proposed for the [[New Jersey Devils]].  However, it is unlikely that this part of the road will ever become a freeway.

{{njsr box|before=20|beforeother=|title=21|after=US 22|afterother=}}

[[Category:State Routes in New Jersey|021]]</text>
    </revision>
