  <page>
    <title>Echemus</title>
    <id>83577</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35438709</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-16T20:02:57Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Ravenous</username>
        <id>296838</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>added mention of herodotus</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], '''Echemus''' was the king of [[Arcadia]]. He succeeded [[Lycurgus]], and married [[Timandra (mythology)|Timandra]], daughter of [[Leda (mythology)|Leda]] and [[Tyndareus]] of [[Sparta]]. 

Timandra bore him a son, Laodocus, before deserting Echemus for [[Phyleus]], the king of [[Doulichium]].

After the death of [[Eurystheus]], [[Hyllus]] led the [[Heraclids]] to attack [[Mycenae]]. Echemus offered himself as the champion of the defending Arcadian forces and killed [[Hyllus]] in single combat, thus forcing the [[Heraclids]] to withdraw.  This story is mentioned by the [[Tegea|Tegeans]] as an example of of their people's bravery in book 9 of ''[[Histories (Herodotus)|The History]]'' by [[Herodotus]]. 

==Reference==
*March, J., Cassell's Dictionary Of Classical Mythology, London, 1999. ISBN 030435161X

{{Greek-myth-stub}}
[[Category:Greek mythology]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
